<p>Italians began voting Sunday and will continue on Monday in a referendum on a constitutional overhaul of the judiciary. The package would separate the career tracks of judges and public prosecutors, split the High Court of the Judiciary into two bodies, and create a High Disciplinary Court. The vote has also become a direct political test for Prime Minister Giorgia Meloni, with several outlets describing it as a plebiscite on her leadership as she looks for new momentum for her government. Supporters say the changes would modernize Italy’s justice system and draw a clearer line between judicial roles. Critics say the proposal is complex, many voters do not fully understand it, and it could weaken judicial independence. The campaign has unfolded in a sharply polarized atmosphere, and the result could shape both Meloni’s standing at home and the future of institutional reform in Italy.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/9B7Jv1XVQU92p9c3t8SdIndH6ngL_hLyBGtZBrsS9Dt4ER4YPydYQp26u4axaeFUDbA9L7877NKd7FfSVja0-O-D9W83rdIyibip7h0Te2t1xPWyf74JQXnZh5WNYZuWwheMzv8bIH5jcQdS_404wF_7rK-Gu6TeYHpdXxJMjIOzpJIxNKeidrEe_jxY65H380f5v9j-Q11I2psAxYi0Od41mwc' alt='Prime Minister Giorgia Meloni of Italy at a campaign event this month.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Two-day vote: Polls opened on Sunday and remain open through Monday, giving Italian voters two days to decide on the constitutional amendment package.</li><li>Complex proposal: The referendum focuses on the structure of the judiciary rather than a single policy, and several reports say that has left many voters unsure what would actually change.</li><li>Polarized campaign: Le Monde described a deeply polarized campaign and a heavy political atmosphere ahead of the vote.</li><li>US dimension: NPR reported that Meloni’s close relationship with President Trump may also factor into the referendum as voters weigh her broader political direction.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Giorgia Meloni and allies: Meloni’s governing camp presents the referendum as a way to regain momentum and push ahead with institutional changes to Italy’s justice system. (<a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/03/22/italy-s-seeks-new-momentum-with-a-referendum-on-judiciary_6751700_4.html'>Le Monde</a>)</li><li>Critics of the reform: Opponents argue that redesigning judicial careers and oversight bodies could weaken judicial independence instead of improving efficiency. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/22/world/europe/italy-referendum-judiciary-meloni.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>Media and analysts: Several outlets frame the ballot less as a technical legal vote than as a high-stakes judgment on Meloni’s political strength after a tense national campaign. (<a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/italys-justice-referendum-high-stakes-test-conservative-premier-131299794'>ABC News</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.npr.org/2026/03/22/nx-s1-5754666/italys-far-right-prime-ministers-political-future-at-stake-in-referendum-vote'>Italy&#x27;s far-right Prime Minister&#x27;s political future at stake in referendum vote</a> - npr.org</li><li><a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/03/22/italy-s-seeks-new-momentum-with-a-referendum-on-judiciary_6751700_4.html'>Italy&#x27;s Meloni seeks new momentum with a referendum on judiciary</a> - lemonde.fr</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/22/world/europe/italy-referendum-judiciary-meloni.html'>Italy’s Leader Wants to Change the Constitution. Italians Don’t Get It.</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/italys-justice-referendum-high-stakes-test-conservative-premier-131299794'>Italy’s justice referendum becomes a high stakes test for conservative Premier Giorgia Meloni</a> - abcnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.reuters.com/world/italys-justice-referendum-tests-pm-meloni-divided-opposition-2026-03-19/'>Italians begin voting on a constitutional amendment that proposes separating the careers of judges and public prosecutors by splitting the High Court of the Judiciary into two bodies, while also establishing a High Disciplinary Court to oversee disciplinary proceedings.</a> - reuters.com</li></ul>